coder › Done. Here is a summary.

## What I changed

**`src/toc.ts`** — implemented `toc(markdown)` to walk the document line by line:
- Skips anything inside a ``` code fence (a line whose content starts with ``` toggles the fence state, so info strings like ` ```ts ` work).
- Outside fences, recognizes an ATX heading as up to three spaces of indent, 1–6 `#` marks, then at least one space, then text. `level` is the number of `#` marks; `text` is the rest with surrounding whitespace trimmed.
- `slugify` lowercases the text and collapses every run of characters that aren't letters or digits into one dash (`/[^a-z0-9]+/g`), then trims dashes from both ends.
- A `usedSlugs` set resolves collisions: a slug already used gets `-2`, `-3`, … appended (the collision loop also steps past any `-N` that is itself taken).

**`src/toc.test.ts`** — added 13 tests covering: empty input; all six levels in order; text trimming of marks/whitespace; rejection of non-headings (no space after `#`, seven hashes, list items, 4-space-indented lines); slugification of punctuation/case/digits; dash trimming and symbol-only slugs; duplicate suffixes `-2`/`-3` (including case/punctuation variants); source ordering; headings inside fences ignored; fences with info strings; a fence-opening line not being a heading; and blank/plain markdown lines.

## Checks

`bun test` → **13 pass, 0 fail** (15 expect calls).

Two failures during development were bugs in my own test expectations, not the implementation (a "# No space…" string that in fact had a space after `#`, and an apostrophe expected not to produce a dash); fixed the tests, and the spec-faithful implementation then passed.

## Decisions the spec didn't pin down

- **Letters/digits are ASCII** (`[a-z0-9]` after lowercasing), so e.g. `é` would become a dash rather than survive. This is the usual slug convention; say the word if you want Unicode letters preserved instead.
- Fences are only ` ``` ` lines (per the spec), not `~~~` tildes or 4-space-indented code blocks.
- Indentation of up to three spaces before `#` is accepted (markdown-correct); four-plus-space lines are not headings.
- A trailing `#` (e.g. `# Title #`) is kept as part of `text`; only the leading markers are stripped, exactly as specified.

